WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.720
Okay guys, in this problem, a contractor is informed that they must align a 230-foot canal

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.400
with concrete to a thickness of half of a foot.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.320
They are allowed an average excess tolerance of 10% on the total volume of concrete.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.760
How many cubic yards must the concrete company be ready to send to the site?

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.679
So this problem is all geometry, so let's get right into it.

00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:33.000
First of all, we know that this thickness is 6 inches, so let's get the square footage

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.960
of the bottom of the canal's concrete.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:48.239
So we can do 6 inches times 1 foot over 12 inches times 10 feet, which will give us 5 square

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:49.760
feet.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.200
Next, we can find the thickness of the concrete required for the sides of the canal and

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.080
remember that there are two of them.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.960
So to do this, we are going to first need to find the hypotenuse.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.560
All we are given is the slope of 2 to 3.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.120
So what we can do here is seeing that it's also given us the height.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.560
We can simply do a ratio here and solve for the width here, which will then help us to

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.920
solve for the hypotenuse.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:36.120
So we can say 2 over 3 equals 15 over x.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:43.439
Solving for x equals 22.5, and now we can use the Pythagorean theorem to say that 15 feet

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:50.760
squared plus 22.5 feet squared equals y squared, which is the height of the square.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:57.719
Which is the hypotenuse.

00:01:57.719 --> 00:02:16.360
We can see that y comes out to 27.4 feet.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.200
So next, we can get the square footage for both sides of the canal, now that we have

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.920
the hypotenuse.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:32.240
So we can say 27.04 feet times the thickness of 6 inches times 1 foot over 12 inches for

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:43.160
our unit conversion times 2 because there are two sides to the canal.

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:48.680
And that's also going to give us 27.04 feet squared.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.599
If you're confused real quick, why that number is the same?

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:59.560
It's just because we have 6 over 12, which is half, and then we are multiplying by 2 again,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.919
so it just works out that way in this case.

00:03:02.919 --> 00:03:07.599
Now step 4, we can get the total volume of concrete required.

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:15.000
So we can sum up the 5 square feet with the 27.04 square feet, and then multiply by the length

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.240
of the canal, which is 230 feet, and even multiply by the thickness of 10 percent we are

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.719
allowed to ship to the site.

00:03:24.719 --> 00:03:29.840
And doing this math, we will get 8,106 cubic feet.

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:49.240
Now the answer needs to be in cubic yards, so our last unit conversion is going to be 8106

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:58.280
times 1 yard cubed over 3 feet cubed, which is going to give us just about 300 yards cubed,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.560
which we can see over here is one of our answers.
